entry('sensory-association','Somatosensory association cortex',meshes('Superior parietal lobule','Supramarginal gyrus','Angular gyrus'),9,'Interprets tactile features, spatial position, size and learned sensory meaning.',bullets=['Astereognosis: inability to recognize an object by touch.','Agraphesthesia: inability to recognize a traced symbol.','Impaired position/body-part recognition is listed in the lecture as “Astatagnosis”.','Abarognosis: impaired discrimination of weight.'],kind='functional',note='The slide calls the deficit “Graphesthesia”; graphesthesia is the ability, while agraphesthesia is its loss. Functional coverage is broader than a single source parcel.')
entry('wernicke','Wernicke’s language region',meshes('Angular gyrus','Supramarginal gyrus','Superior temporal gyrus (Lateral part)'),9,'A posterior temporal–parietal language network supports comprehension.',bullets=['Lecture emphasis: BA 39 and 40, interpretation of spoken and written words.','Classical lesion pattern: fluent but poorly meaningful speech with impaired comprehension.'],aliases=['Receptive aphasia','Sensory speech','Wernicke'],side='left',kind='functional',refs=['ut-language'],note='BA 39/40 alone are not a complete modern definition. Posterior temporal cortex is also important; the colored gyri are regional context, not an exact Wernicke boundary.')
